{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"Eloise >:3c","id":"260820438026813440","is_bot":0,"username":"effervescenteloise"},"author_id":"260820438026813440","channel_id":"739312505465339954","content":"Web takes a deep breath in, and as they do so their skin seems to breathe in as well. While normally they seem to exude a smell, in this instance they seem to absorb the smells of rot and decay, magically pulling small particulate, bacteria and fungus *into* them to prevent the further decay of the corpses. They breathe out over each in turn, and perfectly sterile air envelops the remains, protecting them.","created_at":"2020-08-02T05:33:29.627000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"739355172282826824","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
{"attachments":[],"author":{"display_name":"KnightOfCydonia","id":"436232470242000916","is_bot":0,"username":"knightofcydonia21"},"author_id":"436232470242000916","channel_id":"739312505465339954","content":"\"Along with that a little bit of knowledge of bugs helps! bugs typically find their way into the corpse about a couple hours after death if unprotected and then maggots start appearing within 5-7 days, now I checked this body earlier today and maggots were there... however we came back tonight and they're gone... notice also the ground is damp... the rain washed them away...\"","created_at":"2020-08-02T05:34:42.104000Z","edited_at":null,"id":"739355476273135636","reply_to_id":null,"thread":null,"thread_id":null}
